      "dom_id": "question-3438",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{2}-2x.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ x & ={x}^{2}-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14264628/assets/d34ebdf90ce2b2796e7ad6f06380c78edd43195ee71a59da4a9a3f877b8b9e1f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)={x}^{2}-2x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x([x]-[{x}^{2}-2x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x(3x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {({x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(27-\\frac{81}{4})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
