    {
      "dom_id": "question-17091",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14264628/assets/98d842c0c8af27575d5d31eb30679e4ae66f97e209c48107919f727c404366aa.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=-6+8x-2{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+8x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+3 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=1$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=3.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}x(-6+8x-2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(-6x+8{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(-3x+4{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
