      "dom_id": "question-352184",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ and $y=x.$ Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=x.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =x \\\\ 4x & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=4.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14264628/assets/71912c1b3d6fbd787a34fa6b5b1829ce0a4e1f6c8e12f25430e18e7c525f5492.png)\n\n  \n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}x([2\\sqrt{x}]-[x])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}x(2\\sqrt{x}-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2{x}^{3/2}-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{128}{5}-\\frac{64}{3})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{128\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
